From caee87540aeafa8cbb374efd57847e0e425dec01 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 12:17:27 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 27/32] llama, ggml-metal: start every row-list run on an
 attention block

A slice's cell list now pads with -1 before a run that does not continue
the previous cell, so each run starts on a multiple of the kernels' 32-row
block; the list grows by the padding. The gather-free Metal variant then
applies to any list the chunked pool produces: a block is one consecutive
range (a run ending inside it reads on to the block's end under the mask,
so the host checks the read stays inside the cache) or all padding, which
the kernel skips. The perf tool gains the run-end and two-run layouts.

The aligned lists are built beside llama.cpp's own: get_seq_cells_aligned
walks the same per-slice cell sets, including a slice over several
sequences. A pool so fragmented that an aligned list would be longer than
the cache keeps the plain list, which the general kernel gathers, so the
mask never grows past what llama.cpp already allows.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QA3dWey3RBtBgDz1Tery9Y
